// ============================================================================
// Resolution · REFERENCE TYPES  (entity -> declared type)
//
// The entity->type projections of type_ref_resolves: for each type-bearing entity
// (field, local, parameter, method return, and the typed expressions cast / new /
// method-reference qualifier), the concrete DECLARED type its declaration
// references. These are the core entity-resolution relations.
//
// Each is a one-liner — filter java_type_reference by (context, referenceOwnerKind)
// and join type_ref_resolves on the reference hash. No nested-type disambiguation is
// needed here: our type_ref_resolves is already precise per
// reference (the qualified/nested Q-path resolves Map.Entry vs the collisions at the
// reference level), so a candidate set never reaches this layer.
//
// DEPTH FILTER (col 7 = "0") IS MANDATORY. A generic type reference is a tree:
// the base type is at depth 0, its type arguments at depth >= 1, and ALL of them
// share the same context and owner. Without the depth="0" filter, a field of type
// TriConsumer<Session, Map.Entry<K,…>, Update<V>> would map that one field to
// TriConsumer AND Session AND Entry AND K AND V — so a call on the field would
// resolve against every type argument, flooding the call graph with spurious
// callees. Depth 0 is the entity's actual (receiver) type — the only one a call on
// it dispatches on. Type arguments (depth >= 1) are a separate concern: reachable-
// type tracking lives in type_ref_resolves (all depths), and type-parameter
// binding/substitution is handled by type_arg_binding, not here.
//
// Provenance (client/lib) rides through from type_ref_resolves — a field/param/etc.
// typed to a library type carries prov="lib", the receiver-side external boundary.
//
// java_type_reference (18): 1 context  7 depth  15 typeReferenceOwnerHash(entity)
//   16 referenceOwnerKind  17 typeReferenceUniqueHash(ref)
// ============================================================================

// ── field_type_resolves(Prov, FieldHash, RefHash, TypeHash) ─────────────────
field_type_resolves(prov, field, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "FIELD_TYPE", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, field, "FIELD", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).

// ── local_var_type_resolves(Prov, LocalHash, RefHash, TypeHash) ─────────────
// depth 0 covers the declared type; multi-catch alternatives are separate
// position-0 references, so each caught type is still captured.
local_var_type_resolves(prov, local, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "LOCAL_VARIABLE", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, local, "LOCAL_VARIABLE", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).
// A `var` local has no written type reference, so the rule above yields nothing. Infer
// the type from a direct object-creation initializer (var x = new Holder<String>()): the
// created type — carrying its written type arguments — becomes the local's resolved type,
// which feeds callee resolution AND type-argument binding uniformly. Gated on the isVar
// flag (col 12); only the syntactic new-initializer, never dataflow (var x = factory()
// would need the call's inferred return type — a separate mechanism).
local_var_type_resolves(prov, local, ref, t) :- java_local_variable(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "true", _, _, _, _, _, _, local),
    java_expression("OBJECT_CREATION", _, "LOCAL_VAR_INITIALIZER", "LOCAL_VARIABLE", _, local, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, objExpr),
    object_creation_type_resolves(prov, objExpr, ref, t).

// `for (var x : xs)` — the loop variable's type is the ITERABLE's element type. `var` writes no
// type reference, so the declared-type rule above yields nothing and every call on the loop
// variable was a declared unknown; `for (W w : xs)` worked all along because the element type is
// written down there.
//
// The link is the ENHANCED_FOR_STATEMENT block: the loop variable's parentExpressionLinkHash IS
// the block that owns the iterable expression, so the two are joined without guessing by line or
// by position. The element type is then whatever the iterable's reference binds for
// java.lang.Iterable's own type variable — which type_arg_binding already carries, because it
// climbs a List<W> reference up through Collection to Iterable.
//
// An ARRAY iterable is not covered: it has no Iterable binding, and an array element type is a
// separate mechanism.
// java_local_variable (19): 8 scopeKind 11 isVarInferred 17 parentExpressionLinkHash 18 hash
// edgeRole = ROOT. A chained iterable emits one ENHANCED_FOR_ITERABLE row per link —
// `map.entrySet()` emits both the entrySet call and the `map` receiver under the same block — so
// without the ROOT filter the loop variable is joined to the inner receiver as well, and takes an
// element type from the wrong expression.
foreach_iterable(local, iterExpr) :- java_local_variable(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "ENHANCED_FOR_LOOP", _, _, "true", _, _, _, _, _, blk, local),
    java_expression(_, "ROOT", "ENHANCED_FOR_ITERABLE", "ENHANCED_FOR_STATEMENT", _, blk, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, iterExpr).

iterable_elem_tvar(tv) :- lib_type(_, "java.lang.Iterable", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, it),
    lib_type_parameter(tv, "0", _, _, _, _, it, _).

local_var_type_resolves(prov, local, ref, elem) :- foreach_iterable(local, iterExpr),
    recv_decl_ref(iterExpr, ref, _),
    iterable_elem_tvar(tv),
    type_arg_binding(ref, ity, tv, elem),
    lib_type(_, "java.lang.Iterable", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ity),
    type_prov(elem, prov).
// The library counterpart: the iterable's element is written on a LIBRARY reference
// (`Map#entrySet()` returns `Set<Map.Entry<K,V>>`), so the binding is a lib_type_arg_binding and
// the climb to Iterable happens there.
local_var_type_resolves(prov, local, ref, elem) :- foreach_iterable(local, iterExpr),
    recv_decl_ref(iterExpr, ref, _),
    iterable_elem_tvar(tv),
    lib_type_arg_binding(ref, ity, tv, elem),
    lib_type(_, "java.lang.Iterable", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ity),
    type_prov(elem, prov).

// A RECORD DECONSTRUCTION component — `if (n instanceof Pair(Leaf a, Node ignored)) a.render()`.
// The component binds a variable exactly as `instanceof Leaf l` does, but the two are shaped
// differently in the IR: a bare pattern's type reference is owned by the LOCAL, so the declared-type
// rule at the top of this file finds it, while a record pattern's components emit their
// PATTERN_BINDING_TYPE references against the enclosing RECORD_PATTERN EXPRESSION. The local is
// there (scopeKind = RECORD_PATTERN) and the reference is there; nothing joined them, so every call
// on a deconstructed component was a declared unknown.
//
// Joined on the component's own written type NAME rather than by position: one RECORD_PATTERN
// expression owns one reference per component and they all carry position 0, so position cannot
// separate them. The name can — and when two components share a type the reference denotes that
// same type anyway, so the join stays correct rather than merely unambiguous. Taking the REFERENCE
// (not just the local's potentialQualifiedName) is what keeps type arguments available downstream.
//
// Scoped to the enclosing method so a name is matched inside the method it appears in.
// java_local_variable (19): 1 name 2 variableTypeName 9 scopeKind 14 methodRegistryLinkHash 19 hash
local_var_type_resolves(prov, local, ref, t) :- java_local_variable(_, tname, _, _, _, _, _, _, "RECORD_PATTERN", _, _, _, _, m, _, _, _, _, local),
    java_type_reference(_, "PATTERN_BINDING_TYPE", _, _, _, _, _, "0", tname, _, _, _, _, _, _, expr, "EXPRESSION", ref),
    expr_ultimate_method("client", expr, m),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).

// ── method_param_type_resolves(Prov, ParamHash, RefHash, TypeHash) ──────────
method_param_type_resolves(prov, param, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "METHOD_PARAM", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, param, "METHOD_PARAM", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).

// ── method_return_type_resolves(Prov, MethodHash, RefHash, TypeHash) ────────
// The declared return type — receiver type of a chained call a().b().
method_return_type_resolves(prov, method, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "METHOD_RETURN", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, method, "METHOD", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).

// ── cast_type_resolves(Prov, ExprHash, RefHash, TypeHash) ───────────────────
// (Foo) x — receiver type of ((Foo) x).bar().
cast_type_resolves(prov, expr, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "CAST_EXPRESSION", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, expr, "EXPRESSION", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).

// ── object_creation_type_resolves(Prov, ExprHash, RefHash, TypeHash) ────────
// new Foo() — the created type (constructor target, and the new-expression's type).
object_creation_type_resolves(prov, expr, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "OBJECT_CREATION_TYPE", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, expr, "EXPRESSION", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).

// ── method_ref_qualifier_resolves(Prov, ExprHash, RefHash, TypeHash) ────────
// Foo::bar — the qualifier type carrying the referenced method.
method_ref_qualifier_resolves(prov, expr, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "METHOD_REFERENCE_QUALIFIER", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, expr, "EXPRESSION", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).

// ── Pattern / lambda binding types — the OTHER receiver sources ──────────────
// The bound variables of these expression forms are call receivers too, so their
// declared types are receiver-typing sources just like locals/params:
//   lambda      (Foo f) -> f.bar()                         LAMBDA_PARAMETER_TYPE
//   instanceof  if (o instanceof Foo f) f.bar()            PATTERN_BINDING_TYPE
//   switch      case Foo f -> f.bar()                      SWITCH_TYPE_PATTERN
//   record      o instanceof Person(String name) ...       RECORD_PATTERN_TYPE (matched type)
// All are context on java_type_reference with referenceOwnerKind = EXPRESSION, so
// each is the same depth-0 join as above. (Inferred lambda params — `s -> …` with no
// written type — emit no ref; those need functional-interface target typing, deferred.)

// instanceof pattern: `if (o instanceof Foo f) f.bar()` — the tested type is
// INSTANCEOF_TYPE, and the binding variable f takes that type, so this is the
// receiver source for instanceof-pattern variables (PATTERN_BINDING_TYPE is only
// emitted for record-pattern components, not a bare `instanceof Foo f`).
instanceof_type_resolves(prov, expr, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "INSTANCEOF_TYPE", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, expr, "EXPRESSION", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).

lambda_param_type_resolves(prov, expr, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "LAMBDA_PARAMETER_TYPE", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, expr, "EXPRESSION", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).


pattern_binding_type_resolves(prov, expr, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "PATTERN_BINDING_TYPE", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, expr, "EXPRESSION", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).

switch_type_pattern_resolves(prov, expr, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "SWITCH_TYPE_PATTERN", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, expr, "EXPRESSION", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).

record_pattern_type_resolves(prov, expr, ref, t) :- java_type_reference(_, "RECORD_PATTERN_TYPE", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, expr, "EXPRESSION", ref),
    type_ref_resolves(prov, ref, t).


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RECORD ACCESSORS: the return type comes from the COMPONENT
//
// A record accessor is declared by the language, not written out, so it has no
// java_type_reference at context METHOD_RETURN and method_return_type_resolves has no
// row for it. It resolved as a CALLEE and its result had no type, so anything chained
// onto it resolved to nothing: `o.origin().x()` lost the second hop, and so did
// `o.origin().describe()`, which is what proves the missing piece is the return type
// rather than the callee.
//
// The COMPONENT does have a reference: the parser emits components as fields, at
// context FIELD_TYPE, with the declared type the record header gives them. An accessor
// and its component share a name and an owner, which is the whole of the record
// contract, so this is a join. Reusing the component's own reference keeps consumers
// that read it (type arguments, generic binding) pointing at a node the IR has.
//
// TWO PROVENANCES, and conflating them is why the first draft derived nothing: the
// component is declared on one side while the type it holds may live on the other. A
// client record with a library-typed component is the ordinary case, so the accessor's
// prov and the RESOLVED TYPE's prov are separate variables. The head takes the type's,
// matching the rule at the top of this file.
//
// #885 did the same thing for accessors an annotation processor declares. That rule
// keys on gen_accessor_src (generated-members.dl) and cannot see these: a record's are
// the language's, and arrive as java_method rows of kind RECORD_ACCESSOR.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
rec_accessor_component(prov, m, f) :- method_kind(prov, "RECORD_ACCESSOR", _, m),
    method_decl(prov, name, _, _, m), name != "",
    method_owner(prov, owner, m),
    field_decl(prov, name, _, owner, f).

method_return_type_resolves(tprov, m, ref, t) :- rec_accessor_component(_, m, f),
    field_type_resolves(tprov, f, ref, t).
